==============================================================================
WHICH SITE IS ABI-4 -- necessity and sufficiency, per site
==============================================================================
  NEEDS(s) = green as shipped, RED with site s re-broken
  FIXES(s) = green with the other two re-broken, RED with all three

  site A   NEEDS  30   FIXES   0
    NEEDS: ['abi4_fp8to_0_01', 'abi4_fp8to_0_04', 'abi4_fp8to_0_08', 'abi4_fp8to_0_3C'] ...
    FIXES: []

  site B   NEEDS  10   FIXES  10
    NEEDS: ['abi4_fp16_0p5', 'abi4_fp16_100p0', 'abi4_fp16_1069547520p0', 'abi4_fp16_1073741824p0'] ...
    FIXES: ['abi4_fp16_0p5', 'abi4_fp16_100p0', 'abi4_fp16_1069547520p0', 'abi4_fp16_1073741824p0'] ...

  site C   NEEDS  40   FIXES  10
    NEEDS: ['abi4_fp8to_0_01', 'abi4_fp8to_0_04', 'abi4_fp8to_0_08', 'abi4_fp8to_0_3C'] ...
    FIXES: ['abi4_fp8to_0_7F', 'abi4_fp8to_0_80', 'abi4_fp8to_0_FF', 'abi4_fp8to_1_7C'] ...
    NEEDS(A^B) = 0 []
    NEEDS(A^C) = 30 ['abi4_fp8to_0_01', 'abi4_fp8to_0_04', 'abi4_fp8to_0_08']
    NEEDS(B^C) = 0 []

  READ AS FOLLOWS.  `of32` is PATTERN->VALUE, `bits32` is
  VALUE->PATTERN, and `dtype.bend` declares `Dt.fp16(x: F32)` and
  `Dt.fp8_to(..) -> IO(F32)`.
    B  NEEDS 10, FIXES 10, and NEEDS(B) touches
       no fp8_to row -> the INBOUND crossing, SEPARABLE BY ROW.
    C  FIXES 10 of which 10 that A and B fix nothing
       -> the OUTBOUND crossing, and the only site with a row set of its
       OWN: fp8_decode's EARLY nan/inf returns never build `v`.
    A  FIXES 0, and NEEDS(A) is a SUBSET of NEEDS(C) (30
       shared) -> NOT separable from C by ANY fixture.  The same error
       (`of32` on a value) one call INSIDE the lane, on C's path, and
       reachable only through C.  Load-bearing, so not incidental -- and
       not a seam crossing either.  `abi_gate.py` carries ONE fp8_to row,
       which needs both, which is why its ABI-4 arm could never separate
       them.

  so: B and C are ABI-4 AT THE SEAM, A is ABI-4's error class applied
  INTERNAL to the lane.  All three are load-bearing.  NONE is incidental.

  DIRECTION, BY VALUE -- the wrong answer NAMES the wrong representation
    abi4_fp16_0p5                  CPython 0.5        shipped 0.5        with B broken 0
    abi4_fp16_100p0                CPython 100.0      shipped 100        with B broken 0
    abi4_fp16_1069547520p0         CPython inf        shipped inf        with B broken 1.5
  `Dt.fp16(x: F32)` receives a VALUE, and 1073741824 / 1069547520 are the
  VALUE whose own f32 PATTERN is 2.0 / 1.5 -- so those rows read the
  argument as a pattern BY NAME, which `fp16(1.5) = 0` cannot.

  THE ALTERNATIVE SPELLING (`ALT`: fp8_decode answers a VALUE, the seam's
  of32 removed) is green on 88/98, RED on exactly
  10 rows, and that set IS FIXES(C): True.
  So the conversion is FORCED ONTO THE SEAM, and the reason is structural:
  fp8_decode returns straight from its nan/inf branches WITHOUT building
  `v`, so a conversion placed at its `return` is bypassed by 10 of its 44
  reachable rows.  Measured, and it is why the repair cannot be written
  one call inward.

==============================================================================
LOCALITY -- by row set, by content, and by the tree's own bytes
==============================================================================
  by ROW SET: the repair moved 41/98 rows and 0 of them outside abi4_*  []
  by CONTENT: the repair's diff (20 lines) mentions another ABI's
    tokens: none.  A row-count fence cannot see an entangled
    ARM; this can, because it reads the bytes the arm would apply.
  EVERY ARM, 15 of them: edits naming another ABI: none
  by THE TREE: `shipped` prints 98/98 and the
    only arm that differs from it by a row is ALT (10 rows), so the
    bytes in the repo ARE the repair -- measured, not asserted.  This gate
    patches a copy, so it could not see the tree's own syntax: a dangling
    paren passed 98/98 here until node's exit status was checked.
  by the OTHER GATES: abi_gate rc=0  jsfix_gate rc=0  jsfix shipped 30/30
    all 30 jsfix rows are `Dt.i64_*` and no arm of this repair touches an
    I64 helper, so jsfix MOVING 0 is a THEOREM about its row set and not
    evidence of locality.  Its rc is the only thing claimed for it here.

